          "heading": "Recommended Cool-Season Vegetables",
          "content": "These crops are ideal for early spring (March-April) or fall (August-September) planting:\n1. Lettuce: Quick-growing, harvest in 30-60 days.\n2. Spinach: Hardy, harvest in 40-50 days.\n3. Radishes: Fastest-growing, harvest in 20-30 days.\n4. Peas: Easy and productive, harvest in 60-70 days.\n5. Broccoli: Hardy but needs space, harvest in 60-100 days.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "heading": "Recommended Warm-Season Vegetables",
          "content": "Plant these after the last frost (mid-April to early May) for summer harvest:\n1. Tomatoes: Popular and rewarding, harvest in 60-85 days.\n2. Zucchini/Summer Squash: Prolific, harvest in 40-60 days.\n3. Beans: Easy and soil-enriching, harvest in 50-60 days.\n4. Cucumbers: Fast-growing, harvest in 50-70 days.\n5. Peppers: Relatively easy, harvest in 60-90 days.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "heading": "Additional Tips for Success",
          "content": "- Succession Planting: Rotate cool and warm-season crops to maximize yield.\n- Frost Protection: Use covers or mulch to extend the season.\n- Pest Management: Monitor for pests and use organic solutions.\n- Companion Planting: Pair crops like tomatoes with basil for better results.\n- Start Small: Begin with 3-5 vegetables to avoid overwhelm.",
          "type": "checklist"
        },
        {
          "heading": "Vegetables to Avoid as a Beginner",
          "content": "Avoid challenging crops like cauliflower, eggplant, and sweet potatoes due to specific care needs or long growing seasons that may not suit all Zone 7b microclimates.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "heading": "Uncertainties and Further Research",
