      "description": "A detailed guide on the ideal dimensions and considerations for a beginner's raised bed garden.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Ideal Dimensions",
          "content": "1. **Width: 3 to 4 feet** - Allows comfortable reach from either side without stepping into the bed, preventing soil compaction. Reduce to 2-2.5 feet if accessible from only one side.\n2. **Length: 4 to 8 feet** - Manageable for beginners, balancing space for crops and ease of navigation. Avoid exceeding 10 feet for accessibility.\n3. **Height/Depth: 6 to 12 inches** - Sufficient for most beginner crops like lettuce and herbs. Opt for 18-24 inches for deeper roots or accessibility needs.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "heading": "Additional Design Considerations",
          "content": "- **Shape**: Rectangular or square (e.g., 4x4 feet) for ease of planning and maximizing space.\n- **Pathways**: Allow 2-3 feet between beds for easy access.\n- **Material Costs**: Smaller beds (e.g., 3x6 feet) are budget-friendly for beginners.\n- **Plant Spacing**: A 4x4-foot bed supports square foot gardening for diverse crops.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "heading": "Factors Influencing Dimensions",
          "content": "- **Available Space**: Adjust size for small areas (e.g., 2x4 feet).\n- **Physical Ability**: Taller or narrower beds for accessibility.\n- **Climate/Location**: Smaller beds for short seasons or shade needs.\n- **Crop Choice**: Larger/deeper beds for bigger plants like tomatoes.",
          "type": "text"
        },
