      "title": "Companion Planting for Vegetables: Tomatoes and Basil",
      "content": "This record provides a comprehensive overview of companion planting, with a specific focus on the mutually beneficial relationship between tomatoes and basil. It includes general principles of companion planting for vegetables, detailed benefits and considerations for the tomato-basil pairing, other compatible plants, practical gardening tips, and limitations of current knowledge.",
      "created_at": "2023-10-05T14:35:00Z",
      "tags": [
        "gardening",
        "companion planting",
        "tomatoes",
        "basil",
        "vegetables"
      ],
      "related_records": [],
      "description": "A detailed guide on companion planting for vegetables, emphasizing the benefits and practicalities of growing tomatoes and basil together.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "What is Companion Planting?",
          "content": "Companion planting involves strategically placing plants near each other to achieve beneficial outcomes such as pest control, pollination, nutrient enhancement, space optimization, and flavor enhancement. It is rooted in traditional knowledge with increasing scientific validation.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "heading": "General Principles of Companion Planting for Vegetables",
          "content": "1. Pairing for Pest Control: Marigolds repel nematodes near tomatoes; nasturtiums act as trap crops for aphids; garlic and onions deter pests near lettuce.\n2. Nutrient Sharing: Legumes like beans fix nitrogen for heavy feeders like corn.\n3. Shade and Support: Corn shades lettuce; pole beans use corn as trellises.\n4. Avoiding Negative Interactions: Onions may stunt beans due to competition or allelopathy.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "heading": "Companion Planting for Tomatoes and Basil",
          "content": "Tomatoes and basil are a classic companion pair with mutual benefits:\n- Pest Repellent: Basil deters tomato hornworms, aphids, and whiteflies (limited scientific evidence).\n- Flavor Enhancement: Anecdotal belief that basil improves tomato flavor, unproven scientifically.\n- Growth Benefits: Basil thrives in tomato shade; attracts pollinators.\n- Soil and Environmental Benefits: Basil suppresses weeds; both thrive in similar conditions.\n- Drawbacks: Overplanting can cause competition for resources.\n- Scientific Evidence: Some studies (e.g., 2010 Journal of Economic Entomology) show reduced pest incidence, but more research is needed.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "heading": "Other Companion Plants",
          "content": "- For Tomatoes: Marigolds (pest control), carrots (soil loosening), onions/garlic (pest repellent), borage (pollinators). Avoid potatoes, eggplants, peppers, and brassicas.\n- For Basil: Peppers (pest control), oregano/chamomile (oil content improvement). Avoid rue and sage.",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "heading": "Practical Tips for Planting Tomatoes and Basil",
          "content": "1. Spacing: Plant basil 12-18 inches from tomatoes.\n2. Timing: Start basil after last frost, later than tomatoes.\n3. Soil: Use well-drained, fertile soil (pH 6.0-7.0) with compost.\n4. Watering: Deep water tomatoes; less for basil; mulch to retain moisture.\n5. Harvesting: Pinch basil to prevent flowering; harvest tomatoes as they ripen.",
          "type": "checklist"
        },
